<section xml:id="sec-covcompounds10" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Additional exercises for Chapter 7</title>

    <p>
        <em>These questions were generated by ChatGPT using prompts from Dr. Vranish.  The questions have NOT YET been edited and the answers to the questions have NOT YET been confirmed by Dr. Vranish</em>
    </p>
<exercises xml:id="new-47-exercises">
  <title>7.1: Making a covalent bond</title>

 <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s1-e1">
    <title>Lewis symbol interpretation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Use the nitrogen Lewis symbol shown. How many valence electrons are present, and how many are unpaired in this introductory dot representation?</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s1-e1-question-fig1">
        <caption>Nitrogen atom: Lewis symbol</caption>
        <image source="atom-nitrogen.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Nitrogen atom: Lewis symbol. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. Five valence electrons: one pair and three unpaired electrons.</description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Five valence electrons; three unpaired dots and one lone pair.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s1-e2">
    <title>Sharing versus transfer</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Compare the electron-sharing model of a covalent bond with the electron-transfer model used to describe ionic bonding.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>A covalent bond shares an electron pair; the ionic model transfers electrons and produces oppositely charged ions whose electrostatic attraction holds them together.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s1-e3">
    <title>Duet rule</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Explain why the displayed hydrogen molecule is stable with only two electrons around each hydrogen.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s1-e3-question-fig1">
        <caption>Hydrogen — H₂</caption>
        <image source="hydrogen.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Hydrogen — H₂. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Each hydrogen counts the shared pair toward a filled first shell. Hydrogen follows a duet rather than an octet.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s1-e4">
    <title>Electron budget</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Two neutral chlorine atoms contribute seven valence electrons each. Draw the Lewis structure of their molecule and account for all fourteen electrons.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p><m>\mathrm{Cl_{2}}</m> has one single bond, using two electrons, and three lone pairs on each chlorine, using twelve more.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s1-e4-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Chlorine — Cl₂</caption>
        <image source="chlorine.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Chlorine — Cl₂. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s1-e5">
    <title>Bond prediction</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Using carbon's Lewis symbol and the octet rule, predict its usual number of covalent bonds in a neutral molecule such as methane.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s1-e5-question-fig1">
        <caption>Carbon atom: Lewis symbol</caption>
        <image source="atom-carbon.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Carbon atom: Lewis symbol. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. Four valence electrons, shown unpaired before bonding.</description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Four bonds. Sharing four pairs allows carbon's four valence electrons to participate in an octet.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s1-e5-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Methane — CH₄</caption>
        <image source="methane.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Methane — CH₄. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s1-e6">
    <title>Lone-pair distinction</title>
    <statement>
      <p>In the displayed <m>\mathrm{HCl}</m> structure, distinguish electrons in the bond from electrons located only on chlorine.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s1-e6-question-fig1">
        <caption>Hydrogen chloride — HCl</caption>
        <image source="hydrogen-chloride.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Hydrogen chloride — HCl. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>One pair is shared in the H-Cl bond; three pairs are lone pairs on chlorine.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s1-e7">
    <title>Octet counting</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Use the oxygen molecule shown. Explain why each oxygen counts eight surrounding electrons although the molecule has only twelve valence electrons in total.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s1-e7-question-fig1">
        <caption>Oxygen — O₂</caption>
        <image source="oxygen.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Oxygen — O₂. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Each oxygen counts four electrons in the shared double bond and four in its own two lone pairs. The bonding electrons count toward both octets.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s1-e8">
    <title>Stable atomic shell</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Draw helium's Lewis symbol and explain why helium does not normally need a covalent bond to complete its shell.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>The symbol has two dots forming one pair. Its first shell is already full.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s1-e8-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Helium atom: Lewis symbol</caption>
        <image source="atom-helium.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Helium atom: Lewis symbol. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. Two valence electrons fill the first shell.</description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s1-e9">
    <title>Model correction</title>
    <statement>
      <p>A classmate claims that every visible dot on an atom's Lewis symbol must become a separate bond. Explain the error using nitrogen.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Pairs may remain nonbonding. Nitrogen commonly forms three bonds while retaining one lone pair; its five valence electrons do not require five bonds.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s1-e9-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Ammonia — NH₃</caption>
        <image source="ammonia.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Ammonia — NH₃. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s1-e10">
    <title>Conservation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>When a shared pair is counted toward both atoms' octets, have two new electrons been created? Explain.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>No. Octet counting describes which atoms share existing electrons; it does not change the total electron inventory.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="new-47-01">
    <title>Sharing</title>
    <statement>
      <p>How does covalent bonding differ from the simple electron-transfer model of ionic bonding?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Covalent bonds share electron pairs rather than creating a lattice of transferred-electron ions.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-47-02">
    <title>Lewis symbol</title>
    <statement>
      <p>How many dots belong in a Lewis symbol for chlorine?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Seven.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-47-03">
    <title>Hydrogen duet</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Why does hydrogen normally form one single bond?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>One shared pair gives it a filled two-electron first shell.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-47-04">
    <title>Bond prediction</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Using the octet rule, how many single bonds does neutral oxygen commonly form?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Two, with two lone pairs.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-47-05">
    <title>Nitrogen prediction</title>
    <statement>
      <p>How many bonds and lone pairs are typical for neutral nitrogen in ammonia?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Three bonds and one lone pair.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-47-06">
    <title>Carbon prediction</title>
    <statement>
      <p>How many ordinary single bonds can carbon form in methane?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Four.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-47-07">
    <title>Helium stability</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Why does helium not normally need to form a bond to fill its outer shell?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Its two-electron shell is already full.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-47-08">
    <title>Shared electron count</title>
    <statement>
      <p>How many electrons constitute a single covalent bond?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Two.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-47-09">
    <title>Lone pair meaning</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Distinguish a lone pair from a bonding pair.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>A lone pair is localized on one atom; a bonding pair is shared between atoms.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-47-10">
    <title>Misconception</title>
    <statement>
      <p>If two atoms share a pair, is that pair counted toward the octet of only one atom?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>No. It counts toward the shell completion of both bonded atoms.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
</exercises>

<exercises xml:id="new-48-exercises">
  <title>7.2: Lewis Structures: Basic Rules</title>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s2-e1">
    <title>Water construction</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Draw water's Lewis structure from its valence-electron total. State how many electrons occupy bonds and how many occupy lone pairs.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Eight electrons total: four in two O-H single bonds and four in two oxygen lone pairs.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s2-e1-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Water — H₂O</caption>
        <image source="water.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Water — H₂O. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s2-e2">
    <title>Central atom choice</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Why should carbon, rather than one hydrogen, be central in methane's skeleton?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Carbon can make four bonds; hydrogen normally makes only one and is terminal.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s2-e2-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Methane — CH₄</caption>
        <image source="methane.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Methane — CH₄. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s2-e3">
    <title>Skeleton completion</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Draw ammonia using eight total valence electrons. Identify the electrons placed after its three single bonds are drawn.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Six electrons form the three N-H bonds; the remaining two form one nitrogen lone pair.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s2-e3-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Ammonia — NH₃</caption>
        <image source="ammonia.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Ammonia — NH₃. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s2-e4">
    <title>Halogen allocation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Construct a Lewis structure for <m>\mathrm{CF_{4}}</m>. Explain where the electrons remaining after the four C-F bonds must go.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>There are 32 valence electrons: eight in bonds and 24 in three lone pairs on each fluorine. Carbon has no lone pairs.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s2-e4-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Carbon tetrafluoride — CF₄</caption>
        <image source="carbon-tetrafluoride.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Carbon tetrafluoride — CF₄. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s2-e5">
    <title>Two central atoms</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Draw ethane, <m>\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}}</m>, and show how each carbon achieves an octet without a multiple bond.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>The carbons have a single C-C bond; each also has three C-H bonds. Seven total single bonds use all fourteen valence electrons.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s2-e5-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Ethane — C₂H₆</caption>
        <image source="ethane.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Ethane — C₂H₆. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s2-e6">
    <title>Error diagnosis</title>
    <statement>
      <p>The candidate water drawing shows two O-H bonds and only one oxygen lone pair. Identify the missing feature and its electron-count consequence.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s2-e6-question-fig1">
        <caption>Candidate drawing: water — incomplete</caption>
        <image source="water-incomplete.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Candidate drawing: water — incomplete. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. Deliberately incomplete candidate for an error-diagnosis exercise.</description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>A second oxygen lone pair is missing. The candidate shows six electrons, but neutral <m>\mathrm{H_{2}O}</m> requires eight.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s2-e6-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Water — H₂O</caption>
        <image source="water.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Water — H₂O. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s2-e7">
    <title>Halogen skeleton</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Describe the correct skeleton and lone-pair placement for phosphorus trichloride, <m>\mathrm{PCl_{3}}</m>, using 26 valence electrons.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Phosphorus is central with three single P-Cl bonds and one lone pair; each chlorine has three lone pairs.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s2-e7-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Phosphorus trichloride — PCl₃</caption>
        <image source="phosphorus-trichloride.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Phosphorus trichloride — PCl₃. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s2-e8">
    <title>Counting shared electrons</title>
    <statement>
      <p>In a valid methane structure, how many electrons surround carbon, and how many electrons does carbon own if bonding electrons are divided equally?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Eight surround carbon; carbon owns four.</p>
      <figure xml:id="ch7-s2-e8-answer-fig1">
        <caption>Methane — CH₄</caption>
        <image source="methane.svg" width="70%">
          <description>Methane — CH₄. Bonds are lines; paired dots are lone pairs. </description>
        </image>
      </figure>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s2-e9">
    <title>Hydrogen placement</title>
    <statement>
      <p>A proposed methane skeleton places H between two carbons with two ordinary single bonds. Why is that hydrogen placement inconsistent with the basic rules?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Two bonds would put four electrons around hydrogen, exceeding its two-electron first-shell capacity.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="ch7-new-s2-e10">
    <title>Procedure ordering</title>
    <statement>
      <p>After a single-bond skeleton is drawn, why should the valence-electron budget be checked before adding dots until every atom appears full?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>The total number of available electrons is fixed. Filling shells without tracking the budget can invent electrons or conceal the need for multiple bonds.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>

  <exercise xml:id="new-48-01">
    <title>Total electrons</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Count the valence electrons in <m>\mathrm{H_2O}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Eight.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-48-02">
    <title>Simple structure</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Describe the Lewis structure of <m>\mathrm{HCl}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>One H-Cl single bond; chlorine has three lone pairs.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-48-03">
    <title>Central atom</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Which atom is central in methane, and why is hydrogen not central?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Carbon; hydrogen ordinarily forms only one bond and is terminal.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-48-04">
    <title>Octet check</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Describe bonds and lone pairs in <m>\mathrm{NH_3}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Three N-H single bonds and one nitrogen lone pair.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-48-05">
    <title>Hydrogen limits</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Why is a structure with two ordinary bonds to a neutral hydrogen atom invalid?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>It exceeds hydrogen's two-electron shell capacity.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-48-06">
    <title>Electron budget</title>
    <statement>
      <p>For <m>\mathrm{CH_4}</m>, how many valence electrons are used in the four bonds?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>All eight valence electrons.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-48-07">
    <title>Electron allocation</title>
    <statement>
      <p>A neutral <m>\mathrm{CF_4}</m> structure has four single bonds. From its total valence budget, determine how many electrons remain as lone pairs and where they go.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>There are 32 valence electrons, with 8 in bonds and 24 in lone pairs: three lone pairs on each fluorine, none on carbon.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-48-08">
    <title>Two centers</title>
    <statement>
      <p>Describe the single-bond framework and carbon octets in ethane, <m>\mathrm{C_2H_6}</m>.</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>A C-C bond and three C-H bonds on each carbon give each carbon four bonds and an octet.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-48-09">
    <title>Missing pair</title>
    <statement>
      <p>A water drawing has two O-H bonds and only one oxygen lone pair. What is missing?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>A second oxygen lone pair, needed for the eight-electron budget and oxygen octet.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
  <exercise xml:id="new-48-10">
    <title>Method</title>
    <statement>
      <p>After drawing single bonds in a neutral Lewis structure, how should remaining valence electrons generally be assigned?</p>
    </statement>
    <answer>
      <p>Complete terminal-atom shells first, then place remaining electrons on the central atom, checking total count and octets.</p>
    </answer>
  </exercise>
</exercises>


</section>
